// TEST: Idx Card Fuel Bomb (rejects)
// ============================================================================
// THE NEGATIVE CONTROL §7 C1 ASKED FOR, AND 154 COULD NOT PIN.
//
//   conjunct 1 of Boom did not fold: Static evaluation: nesting depth limit
//   exceeded (512 levels - possible infinite recursion) at (p = 0, q = 0).
//   Every conjunct is folded once per box cell under a budget of 10000 steps
//   and 512 nesting levels, so a conjunct that recurses without a static
//   bound fails at the FIRST cell it is reached at
//
// A conjunct that cannot terminate is a fence FAILURE, not an exclusion. The
// distinction is the whole point: silently treating an unfoldable conjunct as
// "this cell is not a solution" would report a cardinality — a plausible,
// wrong, silent one — for a struct whose solution set nobody has computed.
//
// WHY THIS FILE COULD NOT EXIST UNTIL NOW. 154 documents the finding at
// length: the budget `StaticEval` spent per cell bounded evaluation DEPTH
// while being named and reported as a step count, and 100,000 nested
// evaluator frames overflow the compiler's stack long before the counter
// reaches zero. A fuel bomb in a conjunct therefore killed the compiler with
// an uncatchable StackOverflowException rather than producing the message
// above, so pinning one here would have pinned a crash. 154 substituted an
// undefined-name conjunct — which fails and RETURNS — and pinned the
// witness-cell half of the requirement on that instead. Both halves are now
// pinned, on the two files, and 154 keeps its own contract.
//
// THE BUDGET IS THE PER-CELL ONE (`StaticEval.cellBudget`: 10,000 steps, 512
// levels), not the `let static` folding budget, and the difference is a cost
// model rather than caution. A cell predicate is a boolean over a handful of
// already-bound integers; a few dozen nodes is a generous reading of a real
// one. Spending the full 100,000-step folding budget at each of up to
// `maxBoxCells` cells would be four orders of magnitude of headroom nobody
// asked for, paid for at the box cap.
//
// ONE CELL, NOT EVERY CELL. §6 risk 3 assumed the bomb burns the budget PER
// CELL. It does not: the flat-filter route stops at the first erroring cell,
// so the cost of this program is one exhausted budget, and the coordinates in
// the message are the first failing cell in LEX order — the same commitment
// 154 pins, reached here by a different failure.
//
// `bomb` itself is legal and is not what is refused. It is a well-formed
// static function; nothing in the language promises that every static
// function terminates, and the fence's job is to say so when one does not,
// at a cell the user can look at.
// ============================================================================
static function bomb(n: Int) -> Int = bomb(n + 1)

static struct Boom {
    p: Int<min=0, max=3>,
    q: Int<min=0, max=3>
} where p + q == bomb(0)

let static CARD = idx_card(Boom)

function main() -> Int = CARD
